   "transcript": "Maya: Thanks for making time, Lars, Ingrid. Last time you mentioned the warehouse in Bergen is where the picking errors hurt most?\nLars: Right. We're at about 3% mis-picks there. Each one costs us a return and an angry customer. We want that under 1% by the summer peak.\nMaya: And today that's all handheld scanners?\nIngrid: Handhelds plus paper for the bulky items. That paper part is the problem.\nMaya: That's exactly where our pick-to-light carts help. How many pickers per shift in Bergen?\nLars: Around forty. If it works there we'd look at Stavanger and Trondheim next year.\nMaya: Got it. On budget, do you have a range for a pilot?\nLars: We've set aside roughly 600,000 kroner for this year. Anything above that needs our CFO, Henrik Dahl.\nMaya: Makes sense. Is Henrik usually involved early or only at sign-off?\nLars: Honestly, it's better to get him in early. He'll ask about payback.\nMaya: Then I'll put together a payback model for Henrik based on your 3% error rate and forty pickers. I can have that to you by Friday.\nIngrid: One concern from my side. Our WMS is Manhattan. Last vendor said they integrate and then it took six months.\nMaya: Fair. I'll send you our Manhattan integration case study and connect you with our solutions engineer, Tomás, for a technical call next week.\nIngrid: Good. Tuesday or Wednesday works for me.\nLars: Also, Autostore came in last month with a much bigger proposal. We're comparing.\nMaya: Understood. I'll include a side-by-side in the proposal, focused on what a forty-picker pilot costs versus a full automation project.\nLars: Perfect. If Henrik is happy with the numbers, we could start a pilot in January.\nMaya: Great. So: payback model by Friday, technical call with Tomás Tuesday or Wednesday, then a proposal with the comparison. I'll also update our notes on the timeline.\nLars: Sounds right.",
